@rem Exercises for lesson 01_basics (C++ track). Format: docs/CONTENT_FORMAT.md

@exercise cpp-basics-theory-header
@type theory
@lesson 01_basics
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Which header do you include to use cout and cin?
@answer
<iostream> | iostream | #include <iostream>
@explain
cout and cin are declared in <iostream>.

@exercise cpp-basics-choice-cin
@type choice
@lesson 01_basics
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Which statement reads an integer from the keyboard into the variable age?
@choices
* cin >> age;
- cin << age;
- cin >> &age;
- cout >> age;
@explain
cin >> variable reads into the variable itself; unlike scanf it needs no & and no format string.

@exercise cpp-basics-predict-bool
@type predict
@lesson 01_basics
@difficulty 1
@prompt
What does this code print?
@code
cout << (3 > 2) << " " << (3 < 2) << endl;
@answer
1 0
@explain
cout prints a true comparison as 1 and a false one as 0.

@exercise cpp-basics-predict-reference
@type predict
@lesson 01_basics
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What does this program print?
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void addOne(int &x) {
    x = x + 1;
}

int main() {
    int a = 5;
    addOne(a);
    addOne(a);
    cout << a << endl;
    return 0;
}
@answer
7
@explain
x is a reference to the caller's variable, so both calls change a: 5 + 1 + 1 = 7.

@exercise cpp-basics-predict-value-vs-ref
@type predict
@lesson 01_basics
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What does this program print?
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void f(int x) { x = 100; }
void g(int &x) { x = 200; }

int main() {
    int a = 1, b = 1;
    f(a);
    g(b);
    cout << a << " " << b << endl;
    return 0;
}
@answer
1 200
@explain
f works on a copy, so a stays 1. g receives a reference to b and changes it.

@exercise cpp-basics-fill-swap-ref
@type fill
@lesson 01_basics
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Make swapInts swap the caller's variables by taking references.
@code
void swapInts(int ___ a, int ___ b) {
    int temp = a;
    a = b;
    b = temp;
}
@answer
&
&
@explain
A & after the type makes the parameter a reference to the caller's variable.

@exercise cpp-basics-bug-by-value
@type bug
@lesson 01_basics
@difficulty 2
@prompt
This program should print 6, but it prints 5. Click the buggy line, then type the corrected line.
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void addOne(int x) {
    x = x + 1;
}

int main() {
    int a = 5;
    addOne(a);
    cout << a << endl;
    return 0;
}
@bugline 4
@answer
void addOne(int &x) {
@explain
x is a copy of a. Taking the parameter by reference (int &x) lets the function change the caller's variable.
